      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        "Phantom limb pain (PLP) is a challenging chronic pain syndrome to treat with pharmacologic agents being first line of management. However, when these agents fail to provide pain relief, other interventions must be considered in a clinical setting. Spinal cord stimulation (SCS) has been shown to provide analgesia in PLP, and should be considered by clinicians.",
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "This PRISMA systematic review analyzes the efficacy of SCS for treatment of PLP."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "After review of 12 studies, there are mixed results to base a conclusion on."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "DISCUSSION",
          "content": "While there is some evidence of efficacy, due to the relatively small number of patients in each study, further research is needed to demonstrate the benefits of SCS for PLP."
        }
      ]},
